===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry|historical}} [[oxygen]] [[gas]], as originally thought to be [[air#Noun|air]] [[deprive]]d of {{l|en|phlogiston|t=the [[hypothetical]] [[fiery]] [[principle]] formerly [[assume]]d to be a [[necessary]] [[constituent]] of [[combustible]] bodies and to be given up by them in burning}}. {{defdate|from 1775}}
#: {{synonyms|en|dephlogisticated gas}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Joseph Priestley|authorlink=Joseph Priestley|title=XXXVIII. An Account of Further Discoveries in Air.{{nb...|By the Rev. Joseph Priestley, LL.D. F.R.S. in Letters to Sir John Pringle, Bart. P.R.S. and the Rev. Dr. Price, F.R.S.}}|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions, Giving Some Account of the Present Undertakings, Studies, and Labours, of the Ingenious, in Many Considerable Parts of the World]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:William Bowyer (printer)|W[illiam] Bowyer]] and [[w:John Nichols (printer)|J[ohn] Nichols]]; for Lockyer Davis,{{nb...|in Holbourn}}, printer to the {{w|Royal Society}}|month=March 15 and May 25|year=1775|volume=LXV, part I|pages=387 and 392|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=LhhWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA387|oclc=630046584|passage=&#91;[https://books.google.com/books?id=LhhWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA387 page 387]&#93; As I think I have that ſufficiently proved, the fitneſs of air for reſpiration depends on its capacity to receive the ''phlogiſton'' exhaled from the lungs, this ſpecies may not improperly be called, '''''dephlogiſticated air'''''. {{...}} &#91;[https://books.google.com/books?id=LhhWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA392 page 392]&#93; Upon the whole, I think, it may ſafely be concluded, that the pureſt air is that vvhich contains the leaſt ''phlogiſton'': {{...}} and that there is a regular gradation from '''''dephlogiſticated air''''', through common air, and ''phlogiſticated air'', down to nitrous air; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Ramsay|authorlink=William Ramsay|chapter=The Discovery of ‘Dephlogisticated Air’ by Priestley and by [[w:Carl Wilhelm Scheele|Scheele]]—the Overthrow of the Phlogistic Theory by [[w:Antoine Lavoisier|Lavoisier]]|title=The Gases of the Atmosphere: The History of Their Discovery|location=London|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Publishers|Macmillan and Co.]]; New York, N.Y.: [[w:Macmillan Inc.|The Macmillan Co.]]|year=1896|page=78|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=SJ86AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA78|oclc=457496975|passage=[[w:Joseph Priestley|[Joseph] Priestley]]'s experiments were performed at intervals from August 1774 till March 1775, and at that date it occurred to him to mix with his '''dephlogisticated air''' some nitric oxide over water; absorption took place, and he concluded that he might assume his new air to be respirable.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Philip Ball|authorlink=Philip Ball|chapter=Revolution: How Oxygen Changed the World|title=The Elements: A Very Short Introduction|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=2002|year_published=2004|page=30|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uaBczzC4wvIC&pg=PA30|isbn=978-0-19-284099-8|passage=[[w:Joseph Priestley|[Joseph] Priestley]] never swayed from his firm conviction in the phlogiston theory as long as he lived, and he called his new gas ‘'''dephlogisticated air'''’.}}

